The longest common subsequence (LCS) of two strings is the longest sequence of characters that is found in both strings. The following function implements this using dynamic programming:
```python
def lcs(str1, str2):
    m = len(str1)
    n = len(str2)
    L = [[0]*(n+1) for i in range(m+1)]
    for i in range(1,m+1):
        for j in range(1,n+1):
            if str1[i-1] == str2[j-1]:
                L[i][j] = L[i-1][j-1] + 1
            else:
                L[i][j] = max(L[i-1][j], L[i][j-1])
    return L[m][n]
```
This implementation uses a 2D array `L` to store the length of the longest common subsequence found so far for all prefixes of str1 and str2. It then fills this array iteratively, comparing characters in the input strings and updating the value of L accordingly. Finally, it returns the length of the longest common subsequence which is equal to L[m][n].assistant

